<Docs edit="site/src/pages/help/troubleshooting.astro" title="Updating and troubleshooting" description="Keep Facet up to date, put it back on the panel, and fix the common set up problems." group="Help" current={url('/help/troubleshooting/')} headings={headings}>
  <h2 id="updates">Firmware updates</h2>
  <p>
    The panel checks for a new Facet release every hour, and shortly after it starts. When there is one, Home Assistant
    shows a <strong>Firmware</strong> update on the panel's device page. Nothing installs by itself: press
    <strong>Install</strong> when it suits you. The panel downloads the update over Wi-Fi and restarts on the new version.
  </p>
  <p>
    To check now, tap <strong>Update</strong> in the panel's Settings. The row shows <strong>Checking…</strong>, then
    <strong>Up to date</strong>, the version that is available (the Update sheet opens by itself) or
    <strong>Couldn't check</strong> with the reason under it (no Wi-Fi, the update server did not answer). Home
    Assistant's Firmware update shows the same result.
  </p>

  <h3 id="channels">Update channels</h3>
  <p>
    Which updates the panel is offered depends on its <strong>Update channel</strong>, in the panel's Settings or as a
    select on its device page in Home Assistant:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stable</strong> (the default): the releases we recommend.</li>
    <li><strong>Beta</strong>: newer versions, more often, that may have rough edges.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dev</strong>: a build of the latest code, made each night when something changed. It has passed the
      automatic checks but nobody has tried it on a panel, so it can break things. Pick it to try a fix early.
      Its version is the release it builds on plus the time of its change, such as
      <code>0.5.0-beta5-dev202611041319+abc1234</code>, so each newer dev build shows as an update.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Switching channel offers that channel's latest version, even when it is older than the one on the panel, so you can
    always go back from Dev to Beta or Stable. The <a href={url('/install/')}>Install</a> page offers Stable and Beta
    only.
  </p>

  <h2 id="settling">After an update</h2>
  <p>
    For the first three minutes on a new version, the panel shows <strong>Update settling: don't unplug</strong> with a
    countdown, and Home Assistant shows the same countdown on the Firmware update. Leave the power on until it reads
    <strong>Update confirmed</strong>. If the panel loses power before then, it starts the version it had before, as a
    safety net. Nothing is lost: install the update again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reflash">Re-flashing</h2>
  <p>
    You can always put Facet back on the panel over USB from the <a href={url('/install/')}>Install</a> page. Firmware
    cannot be uploaded from the panel's own web page, and a network flash needs the panel's
    <a href="#update-password">update password</a>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Without erasing</strong>: the panel keeps its Wi-Fi, its settings and its place in Home Assistant. Use this to repair or update it.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>With erase</strong>: the panel starts as new, with no Wi-Fi and a new hotspot password. Home Assistant no
      longer recognises it: delete the old device in Home Assistant, then add the panel again as on the Install page.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="update-password">Flashing over the network</h3>
  <p>
    Each panel has its own <strong>update password</strong>, made when it first starts, so nobody else on your network
    can put firmware on it. You only need it to flash a build of your own over Wi-Fi (ESPHome's <code>esphome upload</code>
    or <code>espota</code>, port 3232). Updates from Home Assistant's <strong>Firmware</strong> entity and USB flashing
    from the Install page don't use it.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>On the panel: <strong>Settings &gt; Update password</strong>, at the end of the list. Hold the row to show it for a minute.</li>
    <li>
      In Home Assistant: <strong>Developer tools &gt; Actions</strong>, pick
      <code>ESPHome: &lt;panel&gt;_facet_update_password</code> and run it with <strong>Return response</strong>
      ticked. The answer is <code>password: ...</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Put it in your ESPHome YAML as <code>ota: password:</code>, or pass it to <code>espota.py --auth</code>. The panel
    never writes it to its log or to a problem report. A panel updated from an older version gets its password on the
    first start of this one. Flashing with erase makes a new password.
  </p>

  <h2 id="wifi">Wi-Fi does not join</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The panels use <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> Wi-Fi. If your router has separate 2.4 and 5 GHz names, pick the 2.4 GHz one.</li>
    <li>Check the password: it is case sensitive.</li>
    <li>
      If the panel cannot reach its saved network for about a minute and a half, it starts its <code>Facet-</code> hotspot
      again, so you can join it from your phone and pick the network again (<code>192.168.4.1</code> if the page does not
      open).
    </li>
    <li>With the panel plugged into a computer, the Install page can set the Wi-Fi again too: press Install, then <strong>Connect to Wi-Fi</strong>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="home-assistant">Not showing in Home Assistant</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Home Assistant finds the panel on your local network. If they are on different networks or VLANs, discovery
      cannot see it: add it by hand under <strong>Settings &gt; Devices &amp; services &gt; Add integration &gt;
      ESPHome</strong> with the panel's IP address (your router lists it).
    </li>
    <li>The panel waits as long as it takes to be added; it does not need restarting.</li>
    <li>Look for <strong>Facet panel</strong> and the end of its address in the discovered devices.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>It asks for an encryption key.</strong> A new panel never needs one: Home Assistant gives it a key of
      its own while adding it. Home Assistant asks only for a panel it knew before that was then installed with
      erase (the panel's key went with the erase). Delete the old panel under <strong>Settings &gt; Devices &amp;
      services &gt; ESPHome</strong>, then add it again from the discovered devices.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="encryption-key">Home Assistant says the encryption key is invalid</h3>
  <p>
    The panel still holds a key Home Assistant no longer has (Home Assistant was restored from an older backup, or
    the panel was added to another Home Assistant). Clear the panel's key, then let Home Assistant give it a new one:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      On the panel, open <strong>Settings &gt; Home Assistant</strong> and hold <strong>Reset pairing</strong> until
      it fills. <strong>Encryption key</strong> then reads <strong>None</strong>. Without the glass:
      <code>curl -X POST -d "{}" -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;key&gt;" http://&lt;panel&gt;/fw/pairing/reset</code> (an API
      key from Settings &gt; API keys, as for every change from outside).
    </li>
    <li>
      In Home Assistant, the panel's ESPHome entry asks to be confirmed again (<strong>Settings &gt; Devices &amp;
      services</strong>, a <strong>Reconfigure</strong> notice saying the panel no longer uses encryption; reload
      the entry if it does not show). Confirm it: Home Assistant connects and gives the panel a new key. The device,
      its entities and their names stay as they were.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="actions">Controls do nothing</h2>
  <p>
    If the panel shows your lights but tapping them changes nothing, Home Assistant is not letting it act. Open the
    panel's device in Home Assistant, press <strong>Configure</strong> and turn on <strong>Allow the device to perform
    Home Assistant actions</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="link">Lists of your devices are empty</h2>
  <p>
    When you choose a light or a sensor for a screen, the panel reads the list from Home Assistant with an address and a
    token of its own. <strong>Settings &gt; Home Assistant</strong> on the panel says whether it has them: <em>Not set
    up</em>, <em>Linked</em> with your Home Assistant's name, or <em>Not reached</em> with the reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    To set them, open <code>http://&lt;the panel's address&gt;/link</code> on a phone or computer (Settings &gt; Home
    Assistant shows it as a QR code while the panel is not linked). Enter your Home Assistant's address and a
    long-lived access token, and press <strong>Link</strong>. The page tells you when the panel is linked, or why not.
    Give each panel a Home Assistant user of its own, not an administrator, and create the token while logged in as
    that user.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Create a token</strong> on the link page opens the profile of whoever is signed in to Home Assistant in that
    browser. If that is you, the token is yours: sign in as the panel's user first (a private window is easiest), then
    press it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the link page does not open (firmware from before it), set the link from Home Assistant instead. In
    <strong>Developer tools &gt; Actions</strong>, choose the action <code>esphome.&lt;panel&gt;_facet_act</code> and
    set its <code>action</code> field to:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{'{"do": "ha.connect", "url": "http://homeassistant.local:8123", "token": "<the token>"}'}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The panel keeps the address and token when it restarts, and never shows the token back: Settings shows its last
    four characters only.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agents">A script or agent cannot change the panel</h2>
  <p>
    Scripts, automations and agents need <strong>Allow API access</strong> on (the panel's Settings or Home
    Assistant) and a key of their own. Each asks the panel for one with its name, the same way; the panel asks you
    <em>Allow &lt;name&gt;?</em> and the key works once you tap <strong>Allow</strong>. No tap in two minutes is a no.
    A key ends after 30 days unused unless it was asked for as another kind (1 day, or until revoked): ask again.
    <strong>Settings &gt; API keys</strong> lists the keys and revokes them all. <a href={url('/ai/')}>Make screens with AI</a>
    has the prompt to give an agent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">Report a problem</h2>
  <p>
    The panel keeps a problem report: its log from before the last restart, every Wi-Fi and Home Assistant drop with
    how long it lasted and why, the reason for each of the last few restarts and, after a crash, where it crashed.
    Tokens, keys and passwords are taken out before it leaves the panel. To send it with a bug report:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>On the panel, open <strong>Settings &gt; About &gt; Report a problem</strong>. Its <strong>Report page</strong>
      row shows the address of the panel's report page, for example <code>http://192.168.1.50/fw/report</code> (the
      panel's IP address; your router's list of devices shows it too).</li>
    <li>Open that address on a computer on the same Wi-Fi as the panel. The page starts with the steps.</li>
    <li>Copy the report (<strong>Copy report</strong>), or download it (<strong>Download report (.txt)</strong>).</li>
    <li>Open a new issue on GitHub (<strong>Open a GitHub issue</strong>): the bug report form opens with the version
      and board filled in. Paste the report into the <strong>Report</strong> box (or attach the file), and say what
      happened.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The report as plain text is at <code>http://&lt;panel address&gt;/fw/report.txt</code>, for a script or
    <code>curl</code>; its first lines say where to send it. A panel that is not on Wi-Fi yet shows no address: connect
    it to Wi-Fi first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="help">Still stuck?</h2>
  <p>
    Open an issue on GitHub with your panel model and what you see, and its <a href="#report">problem report</a>:
    <a href={url('/contributing/')}>Contributing</a> has the details. The panel's log helps a lot: on the Install page press Install and choose <strong>Logs &amp;
    console</strong>.
  </p>
</Docs>
